 ## Overview
-Pose Recentering transforms a set of camera-to-world (c2w) poses so that their average pose becomes the identity transformation, effectively centering the scene at the origin and aligning the principal axes [S1, S3, unofficial]. This normalization is essential for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and other volume rendering techniques that assume the scene occupies a bounded region (typically $[-1, 1]^3$) and for ensuring numerical stability during optimization [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Rotation Transformation](2279-rotation-transformation.md) and [Translation Transformation](2281-translation-transformation.md) by simultaneously handling both positional centering and rotational alignment in a single coherent step, whereas those siblings handle only one aspect.
+Pose Recentering transforms a set of camera-to-world (c2w) poses so that their average pose becomes the identity transformation, effectively centering the scene at the origin and aligning the principal axes [S1, S3, unofficial]. It simultaneously computes a unified transformation from mean camera centers and axes, distinguishing it from [Rotation Transformation](2279-rotation-transformation.md) and [Translation Transformation](2281-translation-transformation.md) which handle only one aspect [G].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of $N$ camera poses, where each pose is a $3 \times 4$ matrix $P_i$ representing the transformation from camera space to world space. The last column is the translation vector (camera center), and the first three columns are the orthonormal basis vectors (x, y, z axes) of the camera frame.
-**Outputs:** A set of $N$ recentered $3 \times 4$ poses $P'_i$.
